{"hits": {"hits": [{"created": "2008-02-11T00:00:00+00:00", "updated": "2023-03-10T01:10:35.741567+00:00", "links": {"bibtex": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/773705?format=bibtex", "latex-eu": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/773705?format=latex-eu", "latex-us": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/773705?format=latex-us", "json": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/773705?format=json", "json-expanded": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/773705?format=json-expanded", "cv": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/773705?format=cv", "citations": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A773705"}, "metadata": {"authors": [{"full_name": "Balitsky, Ian"}, {"full_name": "Chirilli, Giovanni A."}], "publication_info": [{"journal_volume": "77", "artid": "014019", "year": 2008, "journal_title": "Phys.Rev.D"}], "texkeys": ["Balitsky:2007feb", "Balitsky:2008zza"], "abstracts": [{"value": "The small-$x$ deep inelastic scattering in the saturation region is governed by the non-linear evolution of Wilson-line operators. In the leading logarithmic approximation it is given by the BK equation for the evolution of color dipoles. In the next-to-leading order the BK equation gets contributions from quark and gluon loops as well as from the tree gluon diagrams with quadratic and cubic nonlinearities. We calculate the gluon contribution to small-x evolution of Wilson lines (the quark part was obtained earlier)."}], "control_number": 773705, "dois": [{"value": "10.1103/PhysRevD.77.014019"}], "arxiv_eprints": [{"value": "0710.4330"}], "titles": [{"title": "Next-to-leading order evolution of color dipoles"}, {"title": "NLO evolution of color dipoles"}], "author_count": 2, "document_type": ["article"]}, "id": "773705"}], "total": 1}, "links": {"self": "https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?sort=mostcited&q=eprint%200710.4330&size=3&page=1&fields=control_number,titles.title,authors.full_name,author_count,publication_info,arxiv_eprints.value,dois.value